<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Atzele</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latgalian borderland in north-eastern Latvia, contested between the crusaders and the Rus' of Pskov in the early 13th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Atzele, called Adzele in Latvian and Ochela in Rus' sources, lay in the north-eastern corner of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aluksne">Alūksne</a>. It was a Latgalian district bordering Estonian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ugandi">Ugandi</a> and the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people farmed and lived in communities centred on hillforts. The district was part of the Rus' tribute zone, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> and Novgorod sent expeditions through it. Little is recorded of its rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Livonian crusaders conquered neighbouring lands, Atzele was divided in 1224 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Riga and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aluksne">Alūksne</a> castle was later built in the area by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1100 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:2.47%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latgalian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Adzele, Ochela</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1162&amp;focus=atzele" aria-label="Atzele on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5228 242 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Atzele lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5606 479h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 498h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5548 498h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Latvia. <a href="/atlas?year=1162&amp;focus=atzele">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

